---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Photo from '../components/Photo.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import AsideNav from '../components/AsideNav.astro';
import { IMG } from '../images';
const path = '/data-sanitization';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Data Sanitization', href: path }];
---
<Base
  title="Data Sanitization & Data Destruction Services"
  description="NIST 800-88 Rev 1 compliant data sanitization in Singapore: onsite and offsite degaussing, crushing, shredding and secure erasure with NSA/CSS-evaluated equipment."
  path={path}
  crumbs={crumbs}
  image={IMG.crushedHdd.src}
>
  <PageHero
    crumbs={crumbs}
    eyebrow="Data destruction services"
    title="Data Sanitization"
    lead="Also known as data destruction: a process that renders access to target data on the media infeasible for a given level of effort."
  >
    <div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn--accent" href="/contact-us#quote">Get a quote</a><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/data-sanitization/guide-to-data-sanitization">Read our guide</a></div>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="section section--surface">
    <div class="wrap with-aside">
      <div class="stack-lg">
        <div class="stack">
          <h2>Data sanitization and destruction services for organizations</h2>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>US NIST 800-88 Rev 1 compliant data sanitization services</li>
            <li>Onsite and offsite degaussing, crushing, shredding and secure erasure</li>
            <li>ISO 9001 and 14001 certified processes with NSA/CSS-evaluated equipment</li>
            <li>Best value guaranteed for both onsite and offsite service (<a href="/data-sanitization/best-price-guarantee">see terms</a>)</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If your organization is in a highly regulated industry, such as finance, insurance, technology, pharmaceuticals,
            communications, energy, healthcare, the public sector or education, proper data sanitization is essential, not an
            option or an afterthought.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read our <a href="/data-sanitization/guide-to-data-sanitization">Data Sanitization Guide</a> or
            <a href="/contact-us">contact us</a> to find out more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="stack">
          <h2>Our data sanitization services</h2>
          <div class="grid">
            <article class="card card--flush">
              <Photo img={IMG.degaussing} alt="Magnet with a magnetic field, illustrating degaussing" class="card-media" />
              <div class="card-body"><h3>Degaussing</h3><p class="muted">Applies a strong magnetic field to randomize the stored magnetic alignment that represents data, making it unreadable.</p></div>
            </article>
            <article class="card card--flush">
              <Photo img={IMG.crushedHdd} alt="Hard disk drive bent in half by a crusher" class="card-media" />
              <div class="card-body"><h3>Crushing</h3><p class="muted">Bends the drive in half to destroy the read/write head, actuator, controller board and storage platters.</p></div>
            </article>
            <article class="card card--flush">
              <Photo img={IMG.ssdShredded} alt="Shredded solid state drives and circuit boards" class="card-media" />
              <div class="card-body"><h3>Shredding</h3><p class="muted">Destroys any storage medium irreversibly, disintegrating each one into hundreds of separate pieces.</p></div>
            </article>
            <article class="card card--flush">
              <Photo img={IMG.erasure} alt="Binary data being swept away, illustrating secure erasure" class="card-media" />
              <div class="card-body"><h3>Erasure</h3><p class="muted">Overwrite, Secure Erase and Cryptographic Erase: old data is securely erased and/or new data is written over it.</p></div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <h2>What is data sanitization (data destruction)?</h2>
          <p>
            Sanitization refers to a process that renders access to target data on the media infeasible for a given level of
            effort (NIST SP 800-88 Rev 1, page iv).
          </p>
          <p>
            The widely accepted NIST 800-88 Rev 1 guidelines classify data sanitization into three levels:
            <a href="/data-sanitization/clear-purge-destroy">Clear, Purge and Destroy</a>. All three are acceptable methods; the
            right choice mostly depends on how your organization classifies the importance of the data.
          </p>
          <p>
            You can read more about the different standards for data sanitization (NIST 800-88 Rev 1, NSA/CSS 9-12, NSA EPL)
            on our <a href="/data-sanitization/standards">Standards</a> page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="split split--top">
          <Photo img={IMG.retiredDrives} alt="Pile of used hard disk drives" />
          <div class="prose">
            <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">Why data sanitization, in short</h2>
            <p><strong>For you and your organization's protection.</strong></p>
            <p>
              It is no longer a matter of if, but how and when an organization's information is breached. End-of-life equipment
              is one of the key routes into an organization.
            </p>
            <p>
              By preventing information breaches when disposing of used IT equipment, organizations close off one more route of
              attack and avoid potential financial losses, lawsuits, or regulatory fines. According to
              <a href="https://www.ibm.com/sg-en/security/data-breach" rel="noopener">IBM's Cost of a Data Breach Report</a>, the
              average cost per record of a data breach in 2021 was USD 161.
            </p>
            <p>
              Two independent studies by
              <a href="https://www.computerworld.com/article/2530795/survey--40--of-hard-drives-bought-on-ebay-hold-personal--corporate-data.html" rel="noopener">Kessler International</a>
              and
              <a href="https://www.blancco.com/press-releases/67-percent-used-drives-sold-ebay-craigslist-hold-personally-identifiable-information-11-percent-contain-sensitive-corporate-data/" rel="noopener">Blancco Technology Group</a>
              found that 40% and 67% of disk drives bought from e-commerce sites still contained sensitive information from their
              previous use, including personal data, financial information, company emails, spreadsheets and photographs.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
          <h2>Why data sanitization, in full</h2>
          <p>
            The modern storage environment is evolving rapidly. Data may pass through multiple organizations, systems and storage
            media in its lifetime, and this is only increasing as storage moves towards distributed, cloud-based architecture. As a
            result, more parties than ever are responsible for sanitizing media effectively, and the potential for sensitive data to
            be collected and retained on media is substantial. This responsibility is not limited to the organizations that create
            or finally store sensitive data: it extends to every intermediary that stores or processes the information along the way.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sophisticated access controls and encryption make it harder for an attacker to reach sensitive information directly. So
            attackers may focus instead on residual data on media that has left an organization without sufficient sanitization.
            Effective sanitization and tracking of storage media are therefore critical to protecting sensitive data against
            unauthorized disclosure, whether that information is on paper, optical, electronic or magnetic media.
          </p>
          <p>
            An organization may dispose of media by charitable donation, internal or external transfer, or recycling in line with
            applicable laws and regulations. Even internal transfers need scrutiny, as legal and ethical obligations to protect data
            such as personally identifiable information (PII) are greater than ever. Whatever the media's final destination, the
            organization must make sure no easily reconstructed data remains on it once it leaves the organization's control, or once
            it is no longer protected at the confidentiality level of the data it holds.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <AsideNav section={path} path={path} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand />
</Base>
